# Two people work this repo through git with different agents, so the shared
# records get edited simultaneously more than anything else. For the two that
# are pure append-per-line, take BOTH sides automatically rather than raising a
# conflict — losing someone's claim or changelog entry is worse than a duplicate
# line, which is visible and trivially tidied.
#
# Deliberately NOT applied to backlog.md or requirements-coverage.md: a clash
# there usually means two people changed the same item's status, and that
# genuinely needs a human to look.
COORD.md    merge=union
CHANGELOG.md merge=union

# The committed design needs NO merge driver: it is saved one file per node and
# one per edge (docs/design/reflow2/, dec:how-the-saved-design-is-laid-out-so-git-merges-it),
# so git's ordinary merge — the one GitHub runs — merges disjoint design changes
# with no conflict. The `docs/design/reflow2.json merge=reflow2` line that
# pointed the old single file at reflow2's --merge-driver came out on 2026-10-03;
# the driver stays available, optional, for a project still on a single file.

# Content-store blobs are immutable and content-addressed: an "edit" is a NEW file
# with a new name, so git never needs to diff one. Marking them binary stops it
# trying — and, the part that matters, stops line-ending conversion silently
# CORRUPTING a PNG on a CRLF checkout (dec:content-manifest).
reflow2-content/** binary
